SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Trump v. Slaughter
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 609 U. S. Part 2 \nPages 422–527 
 \nOFFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nJune 29\, 2026 \nREBECCA A. 
 WOMELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTICE
 : This preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound 
 volume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Decis
 ions\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \np
 io@supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \nPage 
 Proof Pending Publication\n422 OCTOBER TERM\, 2025 \nSyllabus \nTRUMP\, PR
 ESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES\, \net al. v. SLAUGHTER \ncertiorari before j
 udgment to the united states \ncourt of appeals for the district of columb
 ia circuit \nNo. 25–332. Argued December 8\, 2025—Decided June 29\, 20
 26 \nThe Federal Trade Commission (FTC) is a regulatory agency that has \n
 accumulated vast rulemaking\, enforcement\, and adjudicatory powers. \nThe
  FTC's powers belong not to the President or his appointees alone\, \nbut 
 instead to fve Commissioners\, each of whom serves for seven years \nand m
 ay be removed by the President only “for ineffciency\, neglect of \nduty
 \, or malfeasance in offce.” 15 U. S. C. § 41. \nSoon after President T
 rump began his second term in January 2025\, he \nfred the FTC's two Democ
 ratic appointees\, Rebecca Slaughter and Al-\nvaro Bedoya. He did not iden
 tify a cause under the statute. He instead \ntold them their “continued 
 service on the FTC [was] inconsistent with \n[his] Administration's priori
 ties” and that they were removed “pursuant \nto [his] authority under 
 Article II of the Constitution.” App. 28. \nSlaughter fled suit against 
 the President and other executive offcials\, \nseeking relief to restore h
 er to offce. She argued that her removal was \nultra vires\, violated the 
 Administrative Procedure Act\, and violated the \nConstitution. The Distri
 ct Court granted Slaughter's motion for sum-\nmary judgment. It acknowledg
 ed that Myers v. United States\, 272 \nU. S. 52\, generally permits the Pr
 esident to remove exec\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us2
 r62_g314.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us2r62_g314.pdf
